The patterns, verified:

```js
// TABLE-DRIVEN — the highest-ROI pattern for pure logic
const sumCases = [[[], 0], [[1], 1], [[1,2], 3], [[-1,1], 0]];
sumCases.forEach(([input, expected]) => {
  test(`sum(${JSON.stringify(input)}) = ${expected}`, () => {
    expect(sum(input)).toBe(expected);      // failure message names THE ROW
  });
});

// MOCKS — recording stubs + dependency injection
const calls = [];
const mockSave = (user) => { calls.push(user); return Promise.resolve({id: 1}); };
async function register(user, save = realSave) { return save(user); }  // DI seam
await register({name: 'Al'}, mockSave);
expect(calls).toHaveLength(1);               // verified: boundary was called once
expect(calls[0].name).toBe('Al');            // with the right payload

// ASYNC — return or await; the runner handles rejection
// BAD:  test('x', () => { fetchThing().then(d => expect(d).toBe(1)); });  // exits BEFORE the assert
// GOOD: test('x', async () => { expect(await fetchThing()).toBe(1); });   // verified: waits

// SETUP/TEARDOWN — fresh state per test
let db;
beforeEach(() => { db = createDb(); });      // each test gets its own world
afterEach(() => { db.close(); });
```

The matcher families (the vocabulary):
- toBe (Object.is — identity/primitives) vs toEqual (deep structural) vs toStrictEqual (deep + type-strict)
- toBeTruthy/Falsy/Null/Undefined — for sentinel checks
- toHaveBeenCalled / toHaveBeenCalledWith / toHaveBeenCalledTimes — mock assertions
- expect(value).resolves / .rejects — promise outcomes

Vitest vs Jest (the modern choice): same API (test/expect/describe/mocks — near-drop-in), but Vitest runs on Vite's transform pipeline: no-Jest-config needed in a Vite app, ESM-native (no Babel interop), faster watch via HMR, and browser-mode testing. Jest: the default in React/TS templates, the bigger ecosystem of reporters/plugins. Same skills transfer — the patterns above are identical in both.
